{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210691,"nonce":"UZY1yCcL3dcGDUFtvtH6XUWZ2v0=","title":"OSR-entry e2e tests race the compile broker under parallel load","message":"e2e_tiered_backedge_enters_osr_entry, e2e_tiered_backedge_enters_osr_entry_in_method and e2e_tiered_backedge_osr_nested_inside_native_caller assert `tier_stats.any(osr_entries \u003e 0)`. That only holds if the background compile finishes and the loop takes the OSR entry before the script ends. lock_osr_test serialises these three against each other but not against the other ~125 tests, so under a loaded machine they fail with \"expected at least one OSR entry\" in roughly half of full-suite runs. Clean with --test-threads=1. Present at c295f05 and 5792bac.\n\nOptions: make the test drive the broker synchronously (drain the compile queue at the back-edge before continuing), or wait for the install in the test before asserting, or run the OSR-stat assertions single-threaded via a dedicated test binary. Output-correctness assertions in the same tests are fine and should stay parallel.","files":null}]}